WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:15.749 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  unravels defining quality is how it  makes you feel nostalgic it takes you on  this journey through the life story of  an elderly woman and it does it by  transporting you into 12 pictures that  hang on the wall in her house  you don't actually play the game inside  these static pictures but instead within  the very scene where each photo was  taken somewhere along the background of  these twelve landscapes you'll find the  subjects of each photograph they're sort  of eternally suspended in their camera  poses and they're assembled in a  towering glitter of fairy lights it  looks beautiful  so it's not like real and it's not quite  supernatural either you're essentially  playing through a memory and what works  so well is how some of these memories  seem so familiar there's a memory of a  day at the seaside another is of a cold  and drizzly trip through the woods these  places and scenes they naturally sort of  kick loose some of your own memories the  ones that bury deep down in your mind  those memories you have that you  treasure those memories you have that  still hurt and those memories that you  have that do both of those things  eventually the game tells you something  really powerful about those memories now  I won't spoil what the message is and to  be honest it doesn't really work by  saying it aloud anyway but I will say  it's pretty uncommon for a game to have  such a helpful and positive thing to say  another is how dazzlingly gorgeous the  game is its depiction of life at ankle  height has impeccable attention to  detail  it's the little things quite literally  that win you over like how the moss  grows on the neglected side of a stone  bench or how the paint at the base of  banisters cracks and forms into a  brittle skin  [Music]  the audio design is even more impressive  unravel features no spoken words no  narration and developer called word  interactive has instead hinged much of  the narrative on the soundtrack  the Gamble has paid off tremendously the  aching sorrow of the violins and  panpipes the bittersweet folk music that  carries this Celtic vibe a little swells  and stirs along with you just so  wonderfully otherwise it's a fairly  standard puzzle platformer at least  mechanically the character you're  looking at now is Yanni an adorable  accident-prone panicky hero made of wool  he is useless insofar as he can't swim  or talk or punch or duck or backflip or  pretty much any other things Mario can  do as Yanni journeys through each of the  game's 12 levels his body leaves a trail  of string this is a problem because if  he walks too far he's reduced to this  gaunt wireframe eventually he can't go  any further  he's tethered to the spots he's tugging  on this remaining thread of string the  way you keep him going is by collecting  balls of wool that are scattered  throughout each world they sort of  temporarily replenish Yanni's body on  occasion you'll need to backtrack  through each level and simplify Yanni's  trail to that's in order to free up some  slack but Yanni's weakness is also his  strength his party trick is throwing a  lasso string out from his arms  spider-man style which allows him to do  all kinds of action-hero things he can  climb over rocks he can abseil down  trees he can swing across perilous  puddles of rainwater conveniently each  level is dotted with lots of wool virani  can attach his string to and thus create  a whole range of tools such as rope  bridges or trampolines most of the  game's obstacles and sadly from the  second level onwards demand a mixture of  these tools to be fashioned in the  correct order  much of unravels charm comes from how  its puzzles and indeed their solutions  are concocted from everyday items a  crushed can of cola is a couch sized  stamp a fallen tree branch is a  makeshift bobsleigh such imagination  spreads into the hazards and obstacles  to a clump of garden weeds is an  impassable thicket and there's this  field vole - I won't go into it but he  becomes your  worst nightmare but if you're curious  about unravel simply because you want to  be challenged this game will come off as  solid but unspectacular aside from a few  memorable exceptions unravels  underpinning mechanics proved to be a  bit too basic to the extent most puzzles  on particularly satisfying or at least  they're not enjoyable enough eventually  solutions can be kind of gathered just  by observing the convenient placement of  knots around you there's a lack of  pondering a lack of experimenting you're  not really stumbling into new problems  you're just going through variants of  old problems and I found that by level-5  the challenges had lost their creativity  to the extent they actually began to  outstay their welcome unravel is really  good at exposing its own problems  particularly because most levels drag on  a little longer than they should and  sometimes unravel doesn't play by its  own rules either for example the length  of your lasso occasionally stretches out  way further than you were expecting but  that's only because at that precise  moment the game needed it too even worse  the distance you can travel before your  string runs out varies wildly there's a  fair counter argument in that had  unravels stuck to the laws of physics  with scientific rigor it would probably  cease to be as fun but for a game with  physical conundrums at its core it needs  at the very least to be consistent with  its own laws you play unravel long  enough a tiny but noticeable distrust  will have built between you and the game  especially since everything else works  so well unravel is a gorgeous storybook  adventure a tale of a doll brought to  life who travels through the faded  memories of his owner but assessed  purely on gameplay it's a bit like Yanni  himself it's alluring and pleasant just  not quite robust enough  [Music]  you  you

